id: k8s.probe.liveness
kind: kubernetes
title: Liveness probe
aliases: liveness, livenessProbe
tags: operations, availability, health
questions:
  - when should a liveness probe fail?
invariant: A failing liveness probe restarts the container; it answers "is this process wedged", never "is a dependency down".
answer_30s: A liveness probe restarts a container that is stuck (deadlock, exhausted pool); it must not fail because a dependency is down, or you get restart storms.
answer_2m: |
  The consequences differ from readiness: a failure here kills the process.
  That means the probe has to be a signal about the process itself -- can it
  still make progress -- not about the world around it. Cascading-failure
  pattern: database slow -> liveness fails -> every replica restarts -> cold
  caches -> database slower. If restart is unlikely to fix it, restart is
  unlikely to be the right remedy; readiness plus alerting is usually the
  correct pair.
when_to_use:
  - long-running processes that can deadlock or leak into wedged state
avoid_when:
  - the failure is in a dependency (that is readiness plus backpressure)
commands:
  - kubectl get pods => RESTARTS climbing is the liveness fingerprint
  - kubectl describe pod api-... => which probe failed and with what exit code
failure_modes:
  - liveness depending on a shared dependency, restarting healthy replicas
  - initialDelay too short for the process to boot, causing crash loop
related:
  - k8s.probe.readiness
  - k8s.probe.startup
  - design.observability
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/
